Q: .NET logging without passing an ILogger around A: <p>First of all, I <strong>strongly recommend</strong> using the service provider infrastructure and to pass the <code>ILogger</code> in an explicit way. This is what the built-int service provider is for!</p>
<p>However, if you stronly feel you sometimes need to have a piece of infrastructure independent of the service provider (as you said: <em>I don't like having to pass a logger object around everywhere</em>) I strongly recommend against singleton (it's beyond the scope of this answer to discuss that).</p>
<p>Following the Single Responsibility Principle, split the responsibility of the logger from the responsibility of its creator (factory). You still don't have to pass anything around, as your clients just use the factory directly:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Logging.ClearProviders();
builder.Logging.AddConsole();

var app = builder.Build();

MyLoggerFactory.SetProvider(
    () =&gt;
    {
        var loggerFactory = app.Services.GetRequiredService&lt;ILoggerFactory&gt;();
        var logger = loggerFactory.CreateLogger(&quot;default&quot;);
        return new MyLogger(logger);
    });

app.MapGet(&quot;/&quot;, 
    () =&gt; 
    {
        var logger = new MyLoggerFactory().Create();
        logger.LogDebug(&quot;hello!&quot;);
        return &quot;Hello World!&quot;;
    } );

app.Run();

public class MyLoggerFactory
{
    private static Func&lt;MyLogger&gt; _provider;

    public static void SetProvider( Func&lt;MyLogger&gt; provider )
    {
        _provider = provider;
    }
    public MyLogger Create()
    {
        return _provider();
    }
}

public class MyLogger
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;

    public MyLogger(ILogger logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public void LogInformation(string message) =&gt; _logger.LogInformation(message);
    public void LogDebug(string message) =&gt; _logger.LogDebug(message);
    public void LogWarning(string message) =&gt; _logger.LogWarning(message);
    public void LogError(string message) =&gt; _logger.LogError(message);
    public void LogCritical(string message) =&gt; _logger.LogCritical(message);
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you still feel this is too much, you can combine the two responsibilities and have your singleton but you still should make its dependency explicit by changing the constructor to public and calling it from your composition root:</p>
<pre><code>var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Logging.ClearProviders();
builder.Logging.AddConsole();

var app = builder.Build();

var loggerFactory = app.Services.GetRequiredService&lt;ILoggerFactory&gt;();
var logger = loggerFactory.CreateLogger(&quot;default&quot;);

MyLogger.Instance = new MyLogger(logger);

app.MapGet(&quot;/&quot;, 
    () =&gt; 
    {
        MyLogger.Instance.LogDebug(&quot;hello!&quot;);
        return &quot;Hello World!&quot;;
    } );

app.Run();

public class MyLogger
{
    public static MyLogger Instance;

    private readonly ILogger _logger;

    public MyLogger(ILogger logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public void LogInformation(string message) =&gt; _logger.LogInformation(message);
    public void LogDebug(string message) =&gt; _logger.LogDebug(message);
    public void LogWarning(string message) =&gt; _logger.LogWarning(message);
    public void LogError(string message) =&gt; _logger.LogError(message);
    public void LogCritical(string message) =&gt; _logger.LogCritical(message);
}
</code></pre>
<p>But, once again, just don't do that, service provider is there to help your classes resolve dependencies to services in an explicit way. Just embrace it, don't try to work it around.</p>
<p>And if you feel passing the ServiceProvider around would be a good option, it's not.</p>

Q: How to tell Jenkins to keep temporary durable sh tasks? A: <p>A Hack I came up with to get the literal script content is to add an exit trap while debugging:</p>
<pre><code>sh(
  label: &quot;My Script&quot;,
  script: &quot;&quot;&quot;
    trap 'echo &quot;=== BEGIN DURABLE SCRIPT ===&quot;;
          cat &quot;$0&quot;;
          echo &quot;=== END DURABLE SCRIPT ===&quot;' EXIT
    &lt;Your Real Script Content Here&gt;
  &quot;&quot;&quot;
)
</code></pre>
<p>Example console output from a test script:</p>
<pre><code>Pipeline] sh (Test Syntax)
+ trap 'echo &quot;=== BEGIN DURABLE SCRIPT ===&quot;;
      cat &quot;$0&quot;;
      echo &quot;=== END DURABLE SCRIPT ===&quot;' EXIT
+ set -euo pipefail
/home/jenkins/agent/workspace/Test/healthmatters@tmp/durable-62a9b9e5/script.sh.copy: line 11: syntax error near unexpected token `done'
++ echo '=== BEGIN DURABLE SCRIPT ==='
=== BEGIN DURABLE SCRIPT ===
++ cat /home/jenkins/agent/workspace/Test/healthmatters@tmp/durable-62a9b9e5/script.sh.copy

trap 'echo &quot;=== BEGIN DURABLE SCRIPT ===&quot;;
      cat &quot;$0&quot;;
      echo &quot;=== END DURABLE SCRIPT ===&quot;' EXIT

set -euo pipefail

while true; do
  { set +x } 2&gt;/dev/null
  break
done
++ echo '=== END DURABLE SCRIPT ==='
=== END DURABLE SCRIPT ===
</code></pre>

Q: Pause and Resume events - Cordova &amp; Angular8 A: <p>You can try using an arrow function inside of setTimeout since it looks like you want the <code>this</code> context for the component.</p>
<pre><code>powerTestSuccess(){
   setTimeout(() =&gt; {
       console.log(&quot;RESUME EVENT RUNNING&quot;);
       console.log(this.pressed);
       if(this.pressed == true){
           this.doSomething();
       } else {
           this.doSomethingElse();
       }
   }, 0);
}
</code></pre>
